# yarn.lock MUST be committed. It was ignored here, which meant:
#   - `yarn install --frozen-lockfile` could never work on a clean clone
#   - the Docker build's `COPY package.json yarn.lock .npmrc ./` failed outright
#   - every machine resolved semver ranges independently, at build time, so no
#     two builds were guaranteed to contain the same dependency tree
# That last one is almost certainly behind the version drift this repo kept
# hitting (react-query pin lost, typescript "latest" resolving to 7.x, Radix
# 1.0 vs 1.1 prop renames).
# package-lock.json stays ignored on purpose - this is a yarn workspace, and a
# stray npm lockfile would silently compete with yarn.lock.
package-lock.json

.agent

CLAUDE.md
CLAUDE.local.md
# Host-specific deploy settings (see scripts/deploy.conf.example)
scripts/deploy.conf

# Generated by @ducanh2912/next-pwa on every build. Committing them means the
# repo carries a stale service worker that is overwritten each build, producing
# permanent diff noise and merge conflicts on a file nobody edits by hand.
apps/hestia/public/sw.js
apps/hestia/public/sw.js.map
apps/hestia/public/workbox-*.js
apps/hestia/public/workbox-*.js.map
apps/hestia/public/fallback-*.js

# TypeScript incremental build cache. apps/hestia/.gitignore already had this,
# but that file only covers its own directory - so packages/*/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
# kept showing up as untracked after any `tsc -p packages/...`.
*.tsbuildinfo
